I just lost a promising Samurai because I didn't believe that F
corpses went bad, although I quickly found out otherwise when I
munched on an old brown mold corpse I found.

"Ulch! That fungoid vegetation was tainted! You feel deathly sick."

(This should perhaps be noted in the instadeath spoiler: not only meat
can be tainted, but the spoiler lists only the meat version of the
message.)

Being unlucky at the time, I was unable to pray, and my initial
attempt (based on a belief that my god would sometimes heal major
problems even if I wouldn't otherwise be allowed to pray) used up most
of my turns before death. My posthumous item ID showed that I didn't
have anything that would have helped anyway.

Why do molds become tainted, but not lichens? Molds are fungi, and
lichens are fungi in a symbiotic relationship with a photosynthesis-
capable partner, usually algae. I don't see why the presence of algae
in the lichen would prevent its decay. The only reason I can think of
that lichens should be immune to decay but not other F would be if
NetHack's variety of lichens had some kind of antibiotic effect, which
I would expect to be able to exploit in some way.

Lizard corpses face a similar logic flaw: why don't they decay? It
seems to be only to allow you to carry one in case of cockatrice
attacks, which doesn't make a lot of sense.



